<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kurigalzu I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kurigalzu I was a Kassite king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> in the early 14th century BCE. He built extensively in the old cities of Babylonia and founded a new royal city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dur-kurigalzu">Dur-Kurigalzu</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. He corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and one of his daughters married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurigalzu-i.jpg" alt="Kurigalzu I" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert William Rogers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E2%80%98Aqar_Q%C5%ABf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kurigalzu I was the 17th king of the Kassite, or third, dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and he died around 1375 BCE. An inscription known as his autobiography names his father as Kadashman-Harbe I. Galzu was the name by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> called themselves, and the king's name may mean Shepherd of the Kassites, as a Babylonian name-list glosses it.</p><p>A second Kassite king, Kurigalzu II, ruled about 45 years later. Regnal numbers were not customary and both kings reigned long, so it is hard to tell for which of them an inscription was made. The later king is better known for campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> than for building.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the Elamite ruler Hur-batila raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and challenged him to battle, Kurigalzu defeated and captured him, then conquered Susiana and advanced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, which was sacked. Two inscriptions bearing his name were found there, and he may have installed Igi-Halki, the founder of a new Elamite dynasty, as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Chronicle P records the campaign out of sequence and credits it to the later king of the same name.</p><p>He kept up diplomacy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His daughter was given to the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, who made several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diplomatic-marriage">diplomatic marriages</a>. Letters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> recall earlier exchanges with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iv">Thutmose IV</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burna-buriash-ii">Burna-Buriash II</a> later reminded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> that Kurigalzu had refused a request from the Canaanites to join an alliance against Egypt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His building works are attested at no fewer than eleven Babylonian cities. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> he rebuilt the Ningal Temple and set fragments of the Ur-Nammu Stela in buildings on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> terrace. Around 1390 BCE he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dur-kurigalzu">Dur-Kurigalzu</a>, the fortress of Kurigalzu, over an older settlement in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. It was the first royal residence to bear its king's name, and it guarded a trade route east across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, the source of lapis lazuli.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, about 170 feet high, still stands on the western outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Rawlinson identified the site in 1861 from its brick inscriptions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seton-lloyd">Seton Lloyd</a> and Taha Baqir began excavating it in 1942. The city covered 225 hectares and held a vast palace and administrative complex called the Palace of the Whole World. The so-called autobiography of Kurigalzu was probably composed in late Babylonian times, and how far it preserves tradition from his reign is not known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kurigalzu I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The treasures are now divided between the British Museum, the Penn Museum and the Baghdad Museum, and the partly restored ziggurat, built by Ur-Nammu and repaired by Ishme-Dagan of Isin, <b>Kurigalzu I</b> and Nabonidus, remains the best-preserved landmark of the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kurigalzu I</b> built a new royal city, Dur-Kurigalzu, with a ziggurat.</blockquote><a href="/state/kassite-babylonia">Kassite Babylonia</a></li></ul></section>
